.TH GCSL-STDLIB 3 2026-10-18 "Peios gcsl 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gcsl-stdlib \- GCSL standard functions and modules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
import path
import fs
import fileroot
import json
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The standard library is embedded in GCSL.
Each file implicitly imports the prelude through a shadowable import unless it begins with #![no_prelude].
The path, fs, fileroot, and json modules require explicit imports.
An import of builtins exposes the Rust-implemented functions directly.
.PP
Pure operations use : result signatures.
Impure operations use -> result signatures and obey GCSL's present-or-future parameter rules.
The signatures below describe the value shapes the operations consume and return.
.SH PRELUDE
.TP
.B len(list): Int
Return the number of list elements.
This operation has special typing and is not a general first-class polymorphic function.
.TP
.B show(value): String
Render a value as GCSL source, quoting strings and field names when needed.
Values containing functions are not supported.
Required futures are awaited under the normal purity rules.
NaN and infinities cannot be rendered as source.
An empty list still needs an expected type when its rendered text is read back.
The minimum Int value's rendered digits do not form an accepted positive integer literal before unary negation.
.TP
.B record(entries: List<Entry<T>>): {*: T}
Make a record with a field for each entry, named by the entry's key and holding its value, its names known only as the program runs.
type Entry<T> = {key: String, value: T}.
The result has a rest-field alone, so no code reads its fields by name; give it to what takes a record of any names, such as exec's env.
A {*: T} expected of the call gives the entries their type, so record([]) works where one is expected.
Each field is named as the program names it, so the record equals one written with the same fields, whatever the entries' order.
A key given twice is a runtime error.
This operation has special typing and is not a general first-class polymorphic function.
.TP
.B int(x: Float): Int
Truncate toward zero.
NaN, infinity, and values outside the Int range fail.
.TP
.B float(x: Int): Float
Convert to the nearest representable Float.
.TP
.B half(n: Int): Float
Return float(n) divided by 2.0.
.TP
.B split(s: String, sep: String): List<String>
Split at each occurrence of a nonempty separator, retaining empty pieces.
An empty separator is an error.
.TP
.B join(parts: List<String>, sep: String): String
Join strings with sep between adjacent parts; an empty list produces an empty string.
.TP
.B starts_with(s: String, prefix: String): Bool
Test whether s starts with prefix, comparing bytes.
.TP
.B ends_with(s: String, suffix: String): Bool
Test whether s ends with suffix, comparing bytes.
.TP
.B trim(s: String): String
Remove leading and trailing Unicode whitespace.
.TP
.B strip_prefix(s: String, prefix: String): String
Remove one matching prefix; otherwise return s unchanged.
.TP
.B strip_suffix(s: String, suffix: String): String
Remove one matching suffix; otherwise return s unchanged.
.TP
.B lines(s: String): List<String>
Split at LF, removing a CR immediately before it.
A final line ending does not add an empty final element; an empty string produces an empty list.
.TP
.B fields(s: String): List<String>
Split at runs of Unicode whitespace, omitting empty pieces.
.TP
.B compare_versions(a: String, b: String): Int
Return -1, 0, or 1 in version order.
Digit runs compare numerically without fixed-width parsing.
For nondigits, tilde precedes the end of a run, then ASCII letters, then other bytes.
Byte order breaks otherwise equal comparisons, so only identical strings compare equal.
No epoch, revision, or filename-suffix processing is applied.
.TP
.B sort_versions(vs: List<String>): List<String>
Return a stable ascending sort in compare_versions order, retaining duplicates.
.TP
.B Methods of String: split, starts_with, ends_with, trim, strip_prefix, strip_suffix, lines, fields, contains(part: String): Bool, compare_version(b: String): Int
Each does what the function of its name does, its receiver the function's first argument; contains tests whether part occurs in the string, comparing bytes, and compare_version is compare_versions.
.TP
.B Methods of List<String>: join(sep: String): String, sort_versions(): List<String>
.TP
.B Methods of List<T>: len(): Int, unique(): List<T>, contains(x: T): Bool
unique keeps the first of each group of equal elements, wherever they are, in order; contains tests whether an element equals x.
Both use total equality, as === does: every field of a record, and secrets by identity.
An element, or x, that holds a function or a future is a runtime error.
.PP
A method a file declares, or sees from another file, takes the place of one of these that applies to the same receiver.
.SH PROCESS FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B exec(argv: List<String>, cwd: String = \(dq\(dq, env: {*: String} = {}, interactive: Bool = false, panic: Bool = true, until: String = \(dq\(dq) -> {status: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String}
Run argv[0] with argv[1..] as literal arguments, without a shell.
No word and no variable is a secret: exec_secret gives a program secrets.
Search PATH if the program name contains no slash, a PATH given in env included.
Run in cwd, relative to the host working directory, or in that directory for the empty string.
Inherit the host environment with the variables of env set over it.
Supply null standard input.
Capture standard output and standard error in memory.
Decode captured bytes as UTF-8, replacing invalid sequences.
A nonzero status is a runtime error, "`cc` exited with status 1", noted with the trimmed standard error; with panic false it is returned as data instead.
A signal termination is status 128 plus the signal number.
With until, kill the program, and the processes it started in its process group, once a line of either stream contains the text; return the output read so far with status 0.
With interactive, give the program the host's standard input, output and error, and capture nothing; until cannot be combined with it.
A program run with until or under a timeout runs in a process group of its own, so it does not receive a terminal's interrupt.
An empty argument list or failure to start the program is a runtime error.
Every runtime error is reported at the call: exec is track_caller.
.TP
.B exec_live(argv: List<String>, cwd, env, interactive, panic, until) -> {status: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String}
Run as exec does and also copy both output streams to GCSL standard error as complete lines arrive.
A final unterminated line is copied at end of stream.
Captured output is still retained in memory.
Its failure on a nonzero status is not noted with the standard error, which has been copied already.
.TP
.B exec_secret(argv: List<String>, secrets: {*: Revealed<String>}, cwd: String = \(dq\(dq, env: {*: String} = {}, panic: Bool = true, until: String = \(dq\(dq) -> Secret<{status: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String}>
Run as exec does, with the variables of secrets, each a secret revealed with
.BR reveal() ,
set over env as the program's environment variables: the program receives their values.
The result is a secret, whose identity is derived from argv, the options and the secrets' identities.
A nonzero status, unless panic is false, is the runtime error "a program given a secret failed", naming neither the status nor the output; recover and retry handle it.
Nothing is copied to the terminal as it runs, and the program has no terminal.
Files the program writes are not secrets.
.TP
.B sh(script: String) -> String
Run /bin/sh -c with script and return its raw standard output, including a trailing newline.
A nonzero status fails the GCSL run with the script, status, and trimmed standard error, reported at the call.
.TP
.B sh_live(script: String) -> String
Run as sh does with live output copying.
A failure message omits standard error already copied to the terminal.
.TP
.B file(path: String) -> File
Return the file at path, a relative path resolved against the directory of the file the call is written in (the caller's, for the standard library's own code), then normalised lexically: . segments and repeated slashes dropped and each .. removing the segment before it, never above /, with no symbolic link resolved.
Nothing is read, so a missing file is found when it is read.
An empty path is a runtime error.
f.path is its absolute path; under plain GCSL two files are equal when their paths are.
.TP
.B File.read() -> String
Return the file's contents, which must be UTF-8; a file that cannot be read, or is not UTF-8, is a runtime error at the call.
.TP
.B File.exec(...words: String, cwd, env, interactive, panic, until) -> {status: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String}
Run the file as a program with words as its arguments, as exec runs [f.path, ...words] with the same options.
.TP
.B List<File>.file(name: String): File
Return the one file whose path is name or ends with /name; none, or several, is a runtime error at the call.
.TP
.B type FileSet = List<File>, type FilePath = String
Aliases for lists of files and for paths.
A FileSet is a list of loose files; a tree of files is a Dir.
.TP
.B dir(path: String) -> Dir
Return the directory at path, resolved and normalised as file resolves a file's; nothing is read.
.TP
.B working_dir() -> Dir
Return the run's working directory: where a program given no cwd runs, and under pekit a step's own directory, where it writes the files it makes.
Relative paths are never resolved against it.
d.path is its absolute path; under plain GCSL two directories are equal when their paths are.
.TP
.B Dir.file(path: String): File, Dir.dir(path: String): Dir
Return the file or directory at path in d, relative, a leading slash meaning d's root, normalised lexically.
A path that would leave d is a runtime error, and Dir.file needs a path below d; neither reads anything.
.TP
.B Dir.files() -> List<File>
Return every entry of d's tree that is no directory, symbolic links included and never followed, sorted by path.
.TP
.B Dir.exec(...words: String, env, interactive, panic, until) -> {status: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String}
Run words[0] with words[1..] in d, as exec does with cwd d.path.
.TP
.B Dir.sandbox(cwd: String = \(dq/\(dq, env: {*: String} = {}) -> Sandbox
Copy d's tree into a new directory of the run and return a sandbox of it, its commands run in cwd, made if missing, with the environment env by default.
.TP
.B Sandbox.exec(...words: String, cwd, env, interactive, panic, until) -> {status: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String}
Run words[0] with words[1..] as exec does, under bwrap, with the sandbox's copy bound as /, a /proc, a /dev and a /tmp of its own, in new user and pid namespaces, killed with bwrap.
Its working directory is cwd, absolute in the sandbox or relative to its own, or the sandbox's for the empty string.
Its environment is exactly the sandbox's, with env set over it; words[0] is found through that environment's PATH, or the default /bin:/usr/bin.
The network is not shut off.
Without bwrap on PATH, the call is the runtime error "a sandbox needs bubblewrap (`bwrap`), which is not installed".
.TP
.B Sandbox.file(path: String): File, Sandbox.dir(path: String): Dir
Return the file or directory at path in the sandbox, absolute in it or relative to its working directory; a path that would leave it is a runtime error.
Their paths are the host's paths of the copy, and they show what its commands have left so far.
.TP
.B panic(message: String): Never
Stop evaluation with a runtime diagnostic containing message.
No result is returned.
This pure operation can be called from a pure function.
Never is the displayed internal nonreturning type; it cannot be written in a source annotation.
.SH JSON MODULE
.TP
.B json.decode(text: String): T
Decode the JSON document text as a value of the type expected of the call, T.
The type must be expected where the call stands, as in let g: Graph = json.decode(text) or json.decode(text): Graph; otherwise the call is a checking error.
This operation has special typing and is not a general first-class polymorphic function.
A string decodes as a String; a number as an Int if it is written as an integer in the Int range, and as a Float otherwise; true and false as a Bool; an array as a list; and an object as a record.
Each field of a record comes from the key of its name; a missing key or null gives the field's default, and a field without a default must be present.
Keys the type does not name are ignored.
A union decodes as the member that takes the value's kind; no two members may take the same kind, except Int and Float, where an integer goes to the Int.
A record type with a rest-field takes each key it does not name as a field of that name, decoded as the rest-field says; a null value is left out.
Duration, Size, File, Dir, Sandbox, enums, secrets, functions, Partial, and Never cannot be decoded; naming one is a checking error.
The parser follows RFC 8259 strictly and also rejects an object with a duplicate key.
Text that is not JSON, or a document that does not fit the type, is a runtime error at the call that names the line and column, or the path to the value that does not fit, such as units[3].kind.
This pure operation can be called from a pure function.
.SH PATH MODULE
These functions are pure, lexical operations on slash-separated paths.
They do not access the filesystem or resolve symbolic links.
.TP
.B path.is_absolute(p: String): Bool
Return whether p begins with a slash.
.TP
.B path.normalize(p: String): String
Remove empty and dot segments and collapse a preceding ordinary segment for each parent segment.
Retain unresolved leading parent segments in a relative path; do not ascend above an absolute root.
The empty path becomes a dot.
.TP
.B path.join(base: String, rel: String): String
Normalize rel if it is absolute or base is empty; otherwise join with a slash and normalize.
.TP
.B path.dirname(p: String): String
Return the normalized path without its final segment.
.TP
.B path.basename(p: String): String
Return the final normalized segment, or the normalized root or dot when there is none.
.SH FILESYSTEM MODULE
All fs operations are impure and require absolute paths, used as supplied without lexical normalization.
An invalid path or operating-system failure ends the run with a diagnostic.
.TP
.B fs.exists(p: String) -> Bool
Test existence, following symbolic links.
A broken link returns false; inability to check is an error.
.TP
.B fs.read(p: String) -> String
Read the file as UTF-8 text.
Invalid UTF-8 is an error.
.TP
.B fs.mkdir(p: String) -> {}
Create the directory and missing parents; an existing directory is accepted.
.TP
.B fs.copy(from: String, to: String) -> {}
Copy one file's contents and permissions, replacing the destination file.
The destination's parent directory must exist.
.TP
.B fs.write(p: String, contents: String) -> {}
Write through a newly created adjacent temporary file and atomically replace p.
The parent directory must exist.
Replacement uses ordinary new-file permissions, 0666 reduced by the process umask, rather than preserving old permissions.
A symbolic link at p is replaced, not followed.
Failure removes the temporary file.
.TP
.B fs.compose_dir(entries: {*: Dir | File | List<File>}) -> Dir
Make a new directory of the run, removed as temporary directories are, and place each entry of entries at its key, a path in it, / or . meaning the directory itself.
A Dir's tree is copied under its key, a File to its key, and each file of a List<File> under its key by its name; directories merge.
Everything is copied, permissions kept and symbolic links copied as links, so a program writing in the directory changes nothing it was made of.
Two entries placing one path, a key outside the directory, and a file at the directory itself are runtime errors.
.TP
.B fs.temp_dir() -> String
Create a distinct empty directory with mode 0700 and return its absolute path.
The owning run, workspace, or session removes its temporary directories and contents when dropped.
An embedding host can choose an absolute temporary root; that root itself is not removed.
Process termination without normal cleanup can leave temporary directories behind.
.SH FILEROOT MODULE
These members identify source locations, not the subprocess working directory.
.TP
.B fileroot.this
Absolute directory of the source file containing the expression.
.TP
.B fileroot.workspace
Absolute root supplied for the workspace, or the standalone file's directory.
A REPL uses its starting working directory.
.TP
.B fileroot.caller
Absolute source directory of the running function's caller.
.TP
.B fileroot.helper()
Make the running function pass its caller's source directory through to functions it calls.
This supports helpers whose effects should be relative to the original calling source.
Memoized functions account for a caller path when they read it.
.SH SECURITY
Process and filesystem operations use the embedding process's authority.
Lexical path normalization is not a filesystem containment check.
Shell strings are interpreted by /bin/sh; use exec arguments when input must remain literal.
Captured output, read files, lists, and memoization can consume unbounded application memory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gcsl (1),
.BR gcsl (5)
